-- Q4299: Entity Choice and Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Entire amount treated as a tax-free return of shareholder basis","First $150,000 taxed as dividend, remainder as return of basis","AAA distributed first tax-free, then E&P as dividend, then basis return","Entire $200,000 amount treated as a taxable dividend to shareholder"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 1368(c), distributions from an S corporation with accumulated E&P follow a layered ordering: first from the accumulated adjustments account (AAA) tax-free to the extent of stock basis, then from accumulated E&P as taxable dividends, then as return of remaining basis.
Wrong (A): The entire amount cannot be tax-free because the corporation has accumulated E&P from C corporation years that must be distributed as dividends after AAA is exhausted.
Wrong (B): This skips the AAA layer; distributions must first come from AAA before reaching the accumulated E&P layer under the Sec. 1368(c) ordering rules.
Wrong (D): Not all distributions are dividends because the AAA layer representing S corporation earnings is distributed tax-free before reaching the E&P layer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4299;

-- Q4309: Entity Choice and Planning [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The conversion qualifies as a tax-free corporate reorganization","No tax consequences result from the entity conversion process","Shareholders receive a stepped-up basis in partnership interests","The corporation recognizes gain on all appreciated assets at FMV"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Converting a C corporation to a partnership is treated as a complete liquidation under Section 336. The corporation must recognize gain on all appreciated assets as if sold at fair market value, and shareholders recognize gain under Section 331 to the extent FMV exceeds stock basis.
Wrong (A): A C-to-partnership conversion is not a tax-free reorganization; reorganizations under Section 368 apply only to corporate-to-corporate transactions.
Wrong (B): Significant tax consequences arise because the conversion triggers a deemed liquidation with gain recognition at both the corporate and shareholder levels.
Wrong (C): Shareholders do not automatically receive a stepped-up basis without first recognizing gain on the liquidating distribution under Section 331.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4309;

