-- Q4318: Capital Structure Tax Planning [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The physical appearance and color of the stock certificate","The specific state in which the corporation is incorporated","The total number of shareholders in the corporation overall","A fixed maturity date and unconditional obligation to repay"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 385, the Treasury distinguishes debt from equity based on factors including a written unconditional promise to repay, a fixed maturity date, a reasonable interest rate, and the debt-to-equity ratio. A fixed repayment obligation is a hallmark of true debt.
Wrong (A): The physical characteristics of a certificate have no bearing on the debt-versus-equity analysis under Section 385 or case law.
Wrong (B): The state of incorporation is irrelevant to classifying an instrument as debt or equity; the analysis focuses on the economic substance of the arrangement.
Wrong (C): The number of shareholders does not determine whether an instrument is debt or equity; it relates to entity eligibility requirements, not capital structure classification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4318;

-- Q4319: Capital Structure Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The debt exceeds equity by more than a safe harbor 3:1 ratio","The 10:1 debt-to-equity ratio indicates likely equity recharacterization","All shareholder loans are automatically recharacterized as equity contributions","The interest rate charged on the shareholder loan is unreasonably low"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 385, the IRS may recharacterize purported debt as equity when factors such as an excessive debt-to-equity ratio (here 10:1), lack of arm''s-length terms, or absence of a fixed repayment schedule indicate the instrument is equity in substance. Recharacterization converts deductible interest into nondeductible dividends.
Wrong (A): While a 3:1 ratio is sometimes cited as a general guideline, there is no statutory safe harbor ratio; the IRS evaluates the totality of circumstances under Section 385.
Wrong (C): Shareholder loans are not automatically recharacterized; the IRS must evaluate multiple factors before determining that debt should be treated as equity.
Wrong (D): While below-market interest can be a factor, the primary concern here is the extreme 10:1 ratio, not the interest rate charged on the debt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4319;

-- Q4326: Capital Structure Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Amortized and deducted by the issuer over the bond term","Fully deductible by the issuer in the year of issuance only","Not deductible by the issuer under any circumstances whatsoever","Deductible by the issuer only at the bond maturity date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 1272(a), original issue discount is treated as additional interest expense amortized over the life of the bond using the constant yield method. The issuer deducts OID ratably each year, and bondholders include the accrued amount in gross income annually.
Wrong (B): OID cannot be deducted entirely in the year of issuance; Section 1272(a) requires amortization over the bond''s term using the constant yield method.
Wrong (C): OID is deductible as it represents additional interest expense; denying any deduction contradicts the economic substance of the discount as compensation to the bondholder.
Wrong (D): OID is not deferred until maturity; it accrues and is deductible annually over the life of the bond under the constant yield method.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4326;

-- Q4334: Capital Structure Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No tax consequence because the loan is between related parties","Corporation deemed to pay deductible interest; shareholder deemed to receive taxable interest","The entire loan amount is recharacterized as a capital contribution","The shareholder must pay gift tax on the full imputed interest"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 7872, below-market loans between corporations and shareholders result in imputed interest at the AFR. The corporation is treated as paying interest (potentially deductible subject to Section 163(j)), and the shareholder recognizes the interest as taxable income.
Wrong (A): Related-party status does not exempt the loan from tax consequences; Section 7872 specifically targets below-market loans between related parties including corporations and shareholders.
Wrong (C): A zero-interest loan is not automatically recharacterized as a capital contribution; Section 7872 imputes interest rather than reclassifying the entire transaction.
Wrong (D): Gift tax applies to below-market loans between individuals, not corporation-to-shareholder loans, which are treated as constructive distributions rather than gifts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4334;

-- Q4336: Capital Structure Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
