-- Q4354: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Property within the same general asset class designation","Property that is identical to the original converted property","Property meeting the like-kind standard under Section 1031","Property similar or related in service or use to the original"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1033(a), involuntary conversions other than condemnations require replacement property that is similar or related in service or use. This functional use test is stricter than the like-kind standard, requiring the taxpayer to use the replacement in a substantially similar manner.
Wrong (A): The IRS does not apply a general asset class test for involuntary conversions; the standard is the more specific similar-or-related-in-service-or-use test under Section 1033.
Wrong (B): Identical property is not required; the standard permits replacement with property that serves a similar function, not necessarily the exact same type.
Wrong (C): The like-kind standard under Section 1031 applies only to condemnation conversions of real property under Sec. 1033(g), not to casualty or theft conversions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4354;

-- Q4365: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No gain is recognized because gifts are generally nontaxable events","Donor recognizes gain equal to FMV of the obligation minus the basis","The donee assumes responsibility for all of the deferred gain on payments","The installment obligation is cancelled and all gain is permanently forgiven"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453B(a), when an installment obligation is disposed of by gift, the holder recognizes gain equal to the difference between the FMV of the obligation and the holder''s basis. A gift triggers this recognition to prevent permanent avoidance of deferred gain.
Wrong (A): Unlike most gifts, disposing of an installment obligation by gift is a taxable disposition under Section 453B(a), triggering gain recognition for the donor.
Wrong (C): The donee does not assume the deferred gain; the donor must recognize gain at the time of the gift, not defer it to the recipient.
Wrong (D): The obligation is not cancelled by a gift; it continues to exist, but the donor must recognize gain on the disposition under Section 453B.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4365;

-- Q4367: Nontaxable Dispositions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ordinary income reported separately from the installment sale gain","Included as part of the total installment sale gain calculation","Treated as capital gain matching the character of the underlying sale","Treated as a tax-free return of the seller''s original investment basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Interest on an installment note is ordinary income under Section 61, reported separately from the installment gain. The gross profit ratio under Section 453 applies only to principal payments, not interest.
Wrong (B): Interest is not part of the installment gain calculation; the gross profit ratio is applied only to principal portions of each payment received.
Wrong (C): Interest income retains its character as ordinary income regardless of whether the underlying sale produces capital gain.
Wrong (D): Interest represents compensation for the time value of money and is fully taxable; it is not a return of the seller''s investment basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4367;

-- Q4369: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All deferred gain is recognized on the decedent''s final income tax return","Obligation passes to estate or heir with no immediate gain recognition","The obligation is cancelled and all remaining deferred gain is forgiven","The heir must pay all remaining tax liability within 90 calendar days"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453B(c), transfer of an installment obligation at death is not treated as a disposition. The estate or beneficiary takes the decedent''s basis and reports gain as payments are received, though related party sales may trigger acceleration under Section 453(e).
Wrong (A): Death does not trigger immediate recognition of all deferred gain; Section 453B(c) specifically exempts transfers at death from disposition treatment.
Wrong (C): The obligation is not forgiven at death; it passes to the estate or heir, who continues to report gain as installment payments are collected.
Wrong (D): There is no 90-day payment requirement; the heir or estate simply continues reporting gain under the installment method as payments arrive.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4369;

-- Q4371: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No, the taxpayer must elect only one of the two available provisions","Yes, but only Section 1033 involuntary conversion deferral may apply here","No, condemnations are not eligible under either Section 121 or Section 1033","Yes, Section 121 exclusion applies first then Section 1033 on the remainder"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A taxpayer may use both Section 121 and Section 1033 for a condemned principal residence. The Section 121 exclusion ($250,000/$500,000) reduces the gain first, and any remaining gain may be deferred under Section 1033 by reinvesting in replacement property within the replacement period.
Wrong (A): The taxpayer is not required to choose one provision; both sections can apply sequentially to the same transaction to eliminate or defer the entire gain.
Wrong (B): Section 121 also applies to condemnations of a principal residence; it is not limited to voluntary sales, and it provides an exclusion rather than just deferral.
Wrong (C): Condemnations qualify under both provisions; Section 1033 specifically addresses involuntary conversions, and Section 121 applies to any disposition of a principal residence meeting the use requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4371;

-- Q4372: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No tax consequence results from pledging the installment obligation","The taxpayer immediately recognizes the full $80,000 of deferred gain","Gain recognized to the extent of loan proceeds limited to deferred gain","The original installment sale is retroactively voided and fully taxable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 453A(d), pledging an installment obligation as security for a loan causes the loan proceeds to be treated as payments received on the obligation. The taxpayer recognizes gain based on the net loan proceeds, limited to the remaining deferred gain of $80,000.
Wrong (A): Pledging an installment obligation has significant tax consequences; Section 453A(d) treats the loan proceeds as constructive payment on the obligation.
Wrong (B): The taxpayer does not necessarily recognize the full $80,000; gain is limited to the lesser of the loan proceeds or the remaining deferred gain.
Wrong (D): The installment sale is not retroactively voided; the pledging simply accelerates gain recognition to the extent of the loan proceeds under Section 453A(d).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4372;

-- Q4373: Nontaxable Dispositions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Elect deferral on the tax return and purchase replacement property within the statutory period","Report the gain and then file an amended return after buying replacement property","Submit a separate deferral election form directly to the condemning governmental authority","Acquire the qualifying replacement property before the actual condemnation date occurs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1033, the taxpayer must elect deferral on the tax return for the year gain is realized and purchase qualifying replacement property within the replacement period — two years generally, or three years for condemned real property.
Wrong (B): Section 1033 does not require reporting gain and then amending; the election is made on the original return for the year the gain is realized.
Wrong (C): The deferral election is made on the taxpayer''s federal income tax return, not by filing a separate form with the condemning authority.
Wrong (D): The replacement property must be purchased after the conversion, within the statutory replacement period, not before the condemnation occurs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4373;

-- Q4383: Related Party Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Fully deductible as a capital loss in the current tax year","Disallowed under the wash sale rule and added to new share basis","Permanently disallowed with no future recovery of the lost amount","Deferred and deductible in the immediately following tax year only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1091, a loss on the sale of stock is disallowed if substantially identical securities are acquired within 30 days before or after the sale. The January 5 repurchase (21 days later) triggers the rule, and the disallowed $10,000 loss is added to the basis of the replacement shares.
Wrong (A): The loss is not deductible because the repurchase within the 30-day window triggers the wash sale rule under Section 1091.
Wrong (C): The loss is not permanently lost; it is preserved by adding the disallowed amount to the cost basis of the newly purchased shares for future recognition.
Wrong (D): The loss is not automatically deductible the following year; it is recovered only when the replacement shares are eventually sold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4383;

-- Q4384: Related Party Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the 30-day period following the date of the loss sale","Only the 30-day period preceding the date of the loss sale","Both 30 days before and 30 days after the sale (61-day window)","A full 60-day period running only after the date of the sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 1091 applies if substantially identical securities are acquired within the period beginning 30 days before the sale and ending 30 days after — a total 61-day window. Purchases before the loss sale can also trigger the wash sale rule.
Wrong (A): The window is not limited to after the sale; acquisitions in the 30 days before the sale also trigger the wash sale disallowance under Section 1091.
Wrong (B): The window is not limited to before the sale; acquisitions in the 30 days after the sale also trigger the wash sale rule.
Wrong (D): The wash sale window is 30 days (not 60) in each direction, and it runs both before and after the sale, not solely afterward.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4384;

-- Q4392: Related Party Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Long-term capital gain taxed at preferential individual tax rates","Section 1231 gain eligible for net capital gain treatment","Ordinary income due to related party recharacterization rules","Tax-exempt gain excluded from the partner''s gross income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 707(b)(2), when a partner owning more than 50% sells property to the partnership and the property is not a capital asset in the partnership''s hands (here, dealer inventory), the gain is recharacterized as ordinary income rather than capital gain.
Wrong (A): Capital gain treatment is denied because Section 707(b)(2) recharacterizes the gain as ordinary income when the property would be non-capital in the buyer''s hands.
Wrong (B): Section 1231 treatment is also denied; the related party rules override the normal characterization and convert the gain to ordinary income.
Wrong (D): The gain is fully taxable; there is no provision exempting gains on related party sales from income recognition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4392;

-- Q4403: Related Party Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$50,000 gain treated as long-term capital gain on the sale","$50,000 gain classified as Section 1231 gain on business property","$50,000 ordinary income under Sections 1239 and 707(b) combined","$50,000 gain excluded from gross income as a tax-exempt transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): The taxpayer owns more than 50% of the partnership, making them related parties. Under Section 707(b)(2), gain on property that is non-capital in the partnership''s hands is ordinary. Section 1239 also applies since the property is depreciable by the related buyer, independently converting the gain to ordinary income.
Wrong (A): Capital gain treatment is denied because both Section 707(b)(2) and Section 1239 recharacterize gains on depreciable property sales between related parties as ordinary income.
Wrong (B): Section 1231 treatment is overridden by the related party rules; Sections 707(b) and 1239 take precedence and convert the gain to ordinary income.
Wrong (D): The gain is fully taxable as ordinary income; there is no exemption for sales of depreciable property between a partner and a controlled partnership.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4403;

-- Q4975: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It eliminates all state income tax obligations for the individual owners","It converts the owners'' ordinary income into federally taxed capital gains","Individual owners may deduct unlimited state taxes without any special election","Entity pays state tax and owners receive offsetting state credits or exclusions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under PTE tax elections adopted by numerous states as a SALT cap workaround, the entity pays state income tax at the entity level. Individual owners receive a corresponding credit or income exclusion on their state returns, while the entity-level tax is deductible on the federal return without the $10,000 SALT cap limitation.
Wrong (A): The election does not eliminate state taxes; it shifts the payment to the entity level while owners still bear the economic cost through reduced distributions or income adjustments.
Wrong (B): The election has no effect on income character for federal purposes; pass-through income retains its original character as ordinary income, capital gain, or other applicable category.
Wrong (C): Without a PTE election, individual owners are subject to the $10,000 SALT deduction cap under Section 164(b)(6); unlimited deduction is not available without the election.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4975;

