-- Q4980: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$45,000 remaining after applying only a partial $15,000 credit amount","Negative $15,000 liability creating a refund from the excess credit","$0 with a $15,000 carryforward available for use in future tax years","$0 with no carryforward because the excess credit is permanently forfeited"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Corporation N earns a $60,000 credit (12 employees times $5,000) but the nonrefundable credit is limited to the $45,000 tax liability. With no carryforward provision, the excess $15,000 is permanently forfeited, resulting in a $0 liability.
Wrong (A): The full credit (up to the liability amount) applies, reducing the liability to $0; the credit is not limited to only $15,000.
Wrong (B): A nonrefundable credit cannot reduce tax liability below zero or generate a refund; the excess is simply lost when no carryforward exists.
Wrong (C): The problem states the credit has no carryforward provision, so the $15,000 excess cannot be carried to future years and is permanently forfeited.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4980;

-- Q4981: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assigns untaxed nowhere sales to the state where the shipment originated","Removes nowhere sales from both the numerator and denominator of the sales factor","Eliminates the sales factor entirely in favor of property and payroll factors only","Applies exclusively to sales of services rather than tangible personal property sales"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A throwout rule removes nowhere sales from both the numerator and denominator of the sales factor, which increases the apportionment percentage allocated to the remaining taxable states. This contrasts with the throwback rule, which assigns nowhere sales back to the state of origin.
Wrong (A): Assigning nowhere sales to the state of shipment origin describes the throwback rule, not the throwout rule; throwout removes rather than reassigns these sales.
Wrong (C): The throwout rule modifies only the sales factor by removing nowhere sales; it does not eliminate the sales factor or shift reliance to property and payroll factors.
Wrong (D): The throwout rule applies to all types of sales generating nowhere income, including tangible personal property, not just services.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4981;

-- Q4994: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Target is treated as selling all assets at FMV in a deemed asset sale","Target''s historical tax attributes carry over unchanged to the acquirer","The transaction is treated as a tax-free statutory corporate reorganization","The acquiring corporation recognizes gain on the stock purchase price paid"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 338(h)(10), the stock purchase is disregarded and Corporation T is treated as having sold all assets at fair market value in a deemed asset sale. Gain or loss is recognized on old Corporation T''s final return, and Corporation P receives a stepped-up basis in the assets.
Wrong (B): A 338(h)(10) election eliminates carryover of historical tax attributes; the deemed sale creates a new tax basis in all assets, ending the old target''s tax history.
Wrong (C): This is a taxable stock acquisition with an asset-sale election, not a tax-free reorganization; gain is recognized on the deemed sale of assets.
Wrong (D): Corporation P does not recognize gain on the stock purchase; the election recharacterizes the transaction so that tax consequences fall on the target, not the acquirer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4994;

-- Q4995: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Transferor or shareholders must own 50% of voting power or total stock value","Transferor must own at least 80% of the transferee''s total voting stock","Transferor must hold 100% ownership of the controlled transferee corporation","No minimum ownership requirement exists for a Type D reorganization transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 368(a)(1)(D), the transferor corporation or its shareholders must control the transferee, with control defined under Section 304(c) as at least 50% of total voting power or 50% of total value. This is a lower threshold than the 80% control required for other reorganization types.
Wrong (B): The 80% control test under Section 368(c) applies to Type B and Type C reorganizations, not Type D; a divisive Type D requires only 50% control under Section 304(c).
Wrong (C): 100% ownership is not required; the statute requires only 50% of voting power or value, allowing the transferee to have minority shareholders.
Wrong (D): There is a minimum ownership requirement; Section 368(a)(1)(D) explicitly requires control of the transferee as defined in Section 304(c).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4995;

-- Q4998: Entity Formation and Restructuring [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A 338(g) election is available only for targets within an affiliated group","A 338(g) election eliminates all gain recognition on the stock acquisition","A 338(g) election applies exclusively to tax-free corporate reorganization transactions","A 338(g) election triggers gain recognition at both the shareholder and corporate levels"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 338(g), the target is deemed to sell all assets at FMV, but the selling shareholder also recognizes gain on the actual stock sale. This creates double taxation at both the corporate and shareholder levels, unlike a 338(h)(10) election where only the target recognizes gain.
Wrong (A): A 338(g) election is available for any qualified stock purchase, not just affiliated group targets; the affiliated group requirement applies to the 338(h)(10) election instead.
Wrong (B): A 338(g) election does not eliminate gain; it actually increases total tax by triggering recognition at both the corporate and shareholder levels.
Wrong (C): Section 338 elections apply to taxable stock acquisitions, not tax-free reorganizations; reorganizations have their own nonrecognition provisions under Section 368.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4998;

-- Q4999: Entity Formation and Restructuring [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Retain at least 80% of the target''s employees for a minimum of two years","Either continue the target''s historic business or use a significant portion of its assets","Maintain the target as a separate subsidiary entity for at least one full year","File a continuity certification with the IRS within 90 days of the closing date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. 1.368-1(d), the continuity of business enterprise doctrine requires the acquiring corporation to either continue the target''s historic business or use a significant portion of the target''s historic business assets in a business. Either prong satisfies COBE.
Wrong (A): COBE does not impose a specific employee retention threshold; it focuses on continuing the historic business or using historic business assets, not workforce retention percentages.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement to maintain the target as a separate subsidiary; the acquiring corporation may fully integrate the target''s operations as long as one COBE prong is met.
Wrong (D): No separate continuity certification filing is required; COBE compliance is evaluated based on the facts and circumstances and may be challenged on audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4999;
